WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.136
Before anything else, picture this.

00:00:02.536 --> 00:00:12.532
The Linux device tree, your file system and a company org chart are all trees. Interviewers love them because one idea, recursion, solves most tree problems.

00:00:13.732 --> 00:00:14.661
Pause and think.

00:00:15.061 --> 00:00:19.369
In a binary search tree, which traversal prints keys in sorted order?

00:00:19.769 --> 00:00:27.083
Hold your answer; we will check it at the end. And whenever a new word comes up, we stop for a moment and explain it.

00:00:28.283 --> 00:00:35.539
Trees are everywhere: file systems, device trees, PCIe topologies, and the DOM in a browser.

00:00:35.616 --> 00:00:46.657
New word: PCIe. PCI Express: the modern high-speed serial version of PCI. Picture it like this: A multi-lane motorway for expansion cards.

00:00:49.166 --> 00:00:51.233
A tree is like a company org chart.

00:00:51.683 --> 00:00:58.022
There is one root at the top, the CEO. Each person can have reports below them, called children.

00:00:58.472 --> 00:01:00.434
People with no reports are leaves.

00:01:00.884 --> 00:01:05.574
A binary tree means every person has at most two reports: a left and a right.

00:01:06.924 --> 00:01:09.351
A binary search tree adds one rule.

00:01:09.483 --> 00:01:18.376
New word: binary search. Find an item in sorted data by halving the range each step. Picture it like this: Guessing a number with "higher or lower".

00:01:20.026 --> 00:01:24.298
Everything on the left of a node is smaller, and everything on the right is larger.

00:01:24.409 --> 00:01:34.057
New word: node. One point in a graph or tree; also one machine in a cluster. Picture it like this: A city on a map, or one computer in a group.

00:01:35.659 --> 00:01:40.187
So to find six, start at the root eight. Six is smaller, go left to four.

00:01:40.637 --> 00:01:44.538
Six is larger than four, go right. Found it in three steps.

00:01:44.988 --> 00:01:48.924
If the tree is balanced, a search takes about log n steps.

00:01:50.274 --> 00:01:52.410
There are classic ways to walk a tree.

00:01:52.860 --> 00:02:00.082
Depth first goes all the way down one branch before backing up. In-order depth first on a BST visits values in sorted order.

00:02:00.208 --> 00:02:12.248
New word: B S T. Binary Search Tree: a tree where left children are smaller and right children are bigger. Picture it like this: A guessing game: "higher or lower?" at every step.

00:02:13.858 --> 00:02:20.034
Breadth first visits level by level using a queue: eight, then four and twelve, then the bottom row.

00:02:20.075 --> 00:02:26.379
New word: queue. First-in-first-out structure. Picture it like this: A line at a bus stop.

00:02:28.925 --> 00:02:30.574
Keep these costs in mind.

00:02:31.024 --> 00:02:38.802
Balanced trees, like red black trees, give O of log n search, insert, and delete. std map and Java TreeMap use them.

00:02:39.252 --> 00:02:43.780
An unbalanced tree can degrade into a linked list, with O of n operations.

00:02:43.908 --> 00:02:52.523
New word: linked list. A chain of nodes, each pointing to the next. Picture it like this: A treasure hunt where each clue points to the next.

00:02:54.158 --> 00:02:57.641
Tree recursion uses stack memory equal to the height of the tree.

00:02:57.708 --> 00:03:06.775
New word: stack. Last-in-first-out structure; also the memory area holding function calls. Picture it like this: A stack of plates.

00:03:07.175 --> 00:03:16.928
New word: recursion. A function that solves a problem by calling itself on smaller pieces. Picture it like this: Russian dolls: open one to find a smaller one.

00:03:19.458 --> 00:03:23.905
A very common interview bug: checking only a node against its own children.

00:03:24.355 --> 00:03:28.384
In this tree, three is less than six, so it looks fine locally.

00:03:28.834 --> 00:03:35.440
But three sits in the right subtree of five, so it must be greater than five. The tree is not a valid BST.

00:03:35.890 --> 00:03:38.839
The fix is to pass the allowed range down the recursion.

00:03:40.189 --> 00:03:45.785
A trie is a special tree for strings. Each edge is a letter, and paths spell words.

00:03:45.858 --> 00:03:55.483
New word: trie. A prefix tree storing strings letter by letter. Picture it like this: A dictionary's thumb tabs: A → AP → APP.

00:03:55.883 --> 00:04:06.262
New word: edge. A connection between two nodes in a graph; also "the edge" = servers close to users. Picture it like this: A road between two cities.

00:04:07.908 --> 00:04:13.016
Words that share a prefix share the same nodes, like car and cat sharing c and a.

00:04:13.466 --> 00:04:18.551
Search and prefix checks cost O of the word length, no matter how many words are stored.

00:04:19.001 --> 00:04:22.716
Tries power autocomplete and symbol lookup in code search tools.

00:04:24.066 --> 00:04:30.150
Practice Validate BST and Level Order Traversal next, and watch the tree light up step by step.

00:04:30.143 --> 00:04:31.397
Back to the question.

00:04:31.797 --> 00:04:36.104
In a binary search tree, which traversal prints keys in sorted order?

00:04:36.504 --> 00:04:39.105
In-order: left, node, right.

00:04:40.305 --> 00:04:41.176
Quick recap.

00:04:41.576 --> 00:04:43.874
A tree has one root and no loops.

00:04:44.274 --> 00:04:46.933
BST: smaller left, bigger right.

00:04:47.333 --> 00:04:49.725
Most tree answers are a short recursion.
